UID:1eaaa810b11b8525@role-securities-financial.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260822T232151Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20160912
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine’s Bureau of Financial Institutions Wa
 rns Public about  Phony Text Messages Sent to Bank and Credit Union Custom
 ers
DESCRIPTION:Maine’s Bureau of Financial Institutions Warns Public about 
  Phony Text Messages Sent to Bank and Credit Union Customers\nSep 12\, 201
 6\nGARDINER –  Maine’s Bureau of Financial Institutions at the Depart
 ment of Professional and Financial Regulation warns of a scam involving fr
 audulent text messages.  The messages appear to come from a consumer’s 
 bank or credit union and indicate that there is a problem with the consume
 r’s account or debit card.  The scammers urgently request account and o
 ther personal information in order to fix the non-existent problem.  The 
 Bureau of Financial Institutions reminds consumers to look out for this sc
 am and not to divulge bank or credit union account numbers or other person
 al information by text\, phone or email.\nThe customers of several banks a
 nd credit unions received the text message.  The message does not contain
  the name of any particular institution and appears randomly sent to cell 
 phone numbers without targeting consumers at any particular institution. 
  “Banks and credit unions will not text\, call\, or email customers aski
 ng them to divulge account numbers\, pins or social security numbers\,” 
 Superintendent Lloyd P. LaFountain III said.\nLaFountain emphasized that c
 ustomers receiving unexpected calls\, e-mails or text messages should call
  their bank or credit union directly and talk to an employee.  He also no
 ted that customers should always be vigilant to protect their personal inf
 ormation and monitor account statements.\nIf a consumer suspects he or she
  has received a scam text\, the consumer should:\nNot return the text or c
 all the number provided\nNever provide personal account information or oth
 er personal information in response to a text\, call or e-mail.  A bank o
 r credit union does not request personal account information in such a man
 ner.\nThe Bureau of Financial Institutions has a consumer library with hel
 pful information about how consumers can spot and avoid financial scams. 
  Also\, the Bureau’s Consumer Outreach Specialist is av\nhttps://www.mai
 ne.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2022-05-19-maines-bureau-fin
 ancial-institutions-warns-public-about-phony-text-messages
URL:https://www.maine.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2022-05-1
 9-maines-bureau-financial-institutions-warns-public-about-phony-text-messa
 ges
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
